;;; A conclusion's variable that no premise binds, in a rule that never
;;; fired, loaded and answered — **W57**, register row
;;; `W57/assert-var-never-fires` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact `(q a)`, and a rule whose `:match` is empty and whose `:assert`
;;; writes `?y`, which nothing binds: read literally, *every name is a q*.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A conclusion's every variable is bound on every way its `:match` can hold
;;; (Q-M1e2.37), and `()` binds nothing, so the program is refused at load,
;;; whether or not any state fires the rule. Every command that loads it exits
;;; 1 with the loader's line (`ein test`: 2). An empty `:match` is true and
;;; fires once (Q-M1e2.36), so without the refusal the rule would fire at root
;;; with `?y` unbound.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; When an empty `:match` never fired: the program loaded, the rule never
;;; fired, H was empty and R {∅}, exit 0 on every command, with no word of
;;; `?y` (`ein solve FILE` and `ein oracle FILE` at `2eb483e0`, release and
;;; debug alike). A rule that never fired was never checked: an activator
;;; never written, or an `(absent …)` that never holds, read the same. The
;;; engine now refuses it at load, on every command.
;;;
;;;
;;;

(relation q Node :why "{?1} is a q")
(q a)
(rule r ()
  :match ()
  :assert (q ?y)
  :why "every name is a q")
